# Current intent and working requirements

This is an interpretation of [[Sources/Prompt Register|your exact messages]], not a replacement for them.

Your immediate priority is **CC accuracy and verification**. CCs declare what job they promise to do, providing targets for independent held-out conformance tests, deterministic checks, and blind semantic review. Observations, hashes, and fingerprints establish provenance or describe behavior; they do not alone establish correctness. The proposition that CCs improve accuracy remains to be measured.

The semantic verifier sees the declaration and exact output, never the producer's original task input. Protected deterministic fidelity/runtime checks may inspect accepted inputs separately. A verifier must not become a second producer. Mandatory gates remain protected; the planner can propose additional compatible gates.

Abstention is not a wrong answer. Blocking an incorrect candidate is useful prevention; blocking a correct candidate is false rejection. A scientifically correct FAIL or INCONCLUSIVE conclusion is a substantive result. None is interchangeable with a crashed process or a missing model account.

You want the library to cover your full work: current and old designs, code activity, research, experiments, presentations, meeting notes, prompts, decisions, and learning from the one-shot M1 build experiment. Source authority and actual runtime evidence matter more than apparent polish or large test counts.

Use [[Designs/CC Reference|capsule references]] and [[Designs/CC History|the history]] to understand the system. [[Amendments/README|Small coder amendments]] are the implementation handoff, with explicit acceptance evidence and preserved native project identities. Reuse existing JiuwenSwarm/OpenJiuwen components where they improve quality, testing, or effort. Preserve compatible code; make exceptions explicit. Investigate planner visibility before concluding it lacks capsules.

Full CC containerization is the requested design target. Each lab is a VM with its own AI4R, factory and local images. Prove the carrier first with one stateless CC; see [[Designs/Lab Capsule and Factory Design Plan]].

Luna is the default for bounded work. Source extraction, classification, reference drafting, link mapping, and independent reviews can be delegated. Central review resolves authority, contradictions, cross-component contracts, and conclusions. Drafts are not automatically accepted.

## Authority and completion

Your instruction authorizes notebook work and amendment preparation. It does not by itself demonstrate a runtime gain or turn a proposed amendment into an accepted product contract. [[Resources/Current Baseline|Baseline receipts]] pin inspection. Unknowns stay explicit. No unavailable chat is reconstructed and no source original is deleted or relocated.

## Paper2Agent and whole-system improvement direction

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-paper2agent-system-direction.txt|new exact request]] authorizes considering architecture restructuring around Paper2Agent-type flows and eventual whole-system self-improvement. Reusable stages, especially finding and acquiring source, should be plannable capabilities; the whole factory can later be a composed DAG CC. Distinguish its candidate products from its own member CCs and from versioned resources. Reuse the pattern for library preparation and admission evidence without letting a producer grant itself standing.

This expands the long-term objective beyond the earlier narrow improvement discussion. [[Designs/AI4Research Capability Growth]] records current-contract versus desired-behavior gaps and staged proposals; the maintained M1 package has not been amended by these notes. Use Luna for bulk reading/writing; 6.1 Sol is authorized here only for the restructuring and overall self-improvement assessment. Capture important directions, track notes-versus-user gaps, and use [[Designs/Agent Reading and Freshness]] to keep agent reading efficient and source-aware.

## Presentation decision and branch provenance clarification

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-paper2agent-briefing-request.txt|briefing request]] asks for a small, diagram-led reading pack to decide whether Paper2Agent merits deeper investigation. Conceptual fit is already evident: CCs and AI4Research were intended for reusable capability patterns like this. The open question is the practical benefit, cost, and architectural adaptation, rather than whether the idea has any value. Assess flexible CC usage and a coordinator outside individual DAGs alongside ephemeral workers.

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-jiuwenswarm-origin-clarification.txt|origin correction]] is explicit: `jiuwenswarm` on `ai4r_muk` is our modified AI4Research work, not the original upstream JiuwenSwarm. The captured `ai4r_M1` code is another modified implementation checkpoint. Attribute M1 restrictions to our policy/adapter where supported, not to upstream. Compare actual original source and the exact OpenJiuwen dependency pin separately. The concise proposal is in [[Designs/Paper2Agent Briefing/2 Agent architecture]].

## Notebook as source of truth, and the AI4R lab

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-lab-containers-and-source-of-truth.txt|latest message]] asks that this notebook be the one source of truth for current work, and that Muk's statements be written down. The immediate goal is a short Paper2Agent value-and-connection presentation, using [[Designs/Paper2Agent Briefing/1 Decision brief]] and [[Designs/Paper2Agent Briefing/2 Agent architecture]].

Muk also says architectural issues must be resolved. The current `jiuwenswarm` edits are not claimed correct. The earlier design-package targeted parity with the pre-term AI4R project. The future goal is a self-improving AI4R that does a lot better.

New idea: an "AI4R lab" where every CC is a container, many VMs run labs, a central image library vendors CC images to labs, each lab modifies and creates images and modifies itself, and some worktree edits merge back to the master branch. The notebook reads this as candidates returned through central review, never as lab-side promotion. See [[Designs/AI4R Lab and CC Images]] for the reading, the gaps against existing notes, and open questions.

A [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-pasted-architecture-review.txt|pasted review]] was supplied with the message. It prefers one typed workflow runtime over three call modes; the briefing now carries that as a proposal.

## Lab VM, master, and CC as the universal component

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-lab-vm-master-and-cc-as-universal-component.txt|next message]] says each lab is a VM with an AI4R instance and its build resources. Capabilities derive from "master", meaning the latest well-tested branch. The lab's AI4R modifies itself, adds and improves CCs, and adjusts the workflow. Full containerization is likely, with a pilot first. Muk wants CC to be the reusable component for skills, tools, MCP and A2A, and a comparison with the self-improvement goals. See [[Designs/CC as Universal Component]]. Open: who certifies "well tested" outside the lab's reach.

## The Paper2Agent talk

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-paper2agent-presentation-request.txt|request]] asks for a very short, dense, diagram-led talk of about ten minutes. It must include a diagram of information flow showing where leakage and methodology issues arise: researchers use data to make a paper and code, Paper2Agent uses that data and code to make more code, then tests on the same data. It also needs a workflow diagram. The result is [[Designs/Paper2Agent Briefing/presentation/README|the eight-slide deck]]. The leakage points are risks read from the design, not findings in the authors' data.

## Talk revision 4

Muk pasted a [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-pasted-presentation-review.txt|review]] with no comment of their own. It says the talk had drifted into a methodology audit and should explain what Paper2Agent does, how it works, its value to AI4Research, and how both the factory and its generated tools map to capsules, with one balanced limits slide and the pilot protocol in backup. The notebook applied it. One tension is recorded: Muk earlier asked explicitly for a leakage diagram, so one coupling diagram stays in the main talk as the limits slide, with its unsupported claims hedged. Muk can move it to backup. See [[Designs/Paper2Agent Briefing/presentation/README]].

## Talk revision 5

Muk pasted a second [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-pasted-presentation-review-2.txt|review]] and said the notebook may push back where it knows the AI4R planning better. The notebook applied most of it and kept three positions. The worked example (a clustering capability, factory call and product call) is a new slide. Source acquisition is a separate capsule that supplies a pinned snapshot to the factory, which matches Muk's earlier statement that finding and acquiring source should be its own plannable CC; the PRD already bars intake cloning and keeps the Builder offline, so this needs its own authorized path. A capsule may expose one operation or a coherent set, which fits the capsule schema's many unit sizes. The reviewer's request to state the 98.1% metric could not be met from the notes, so the slide says "reported score" and points to the paper. See [[Designs/Paper2Agent Briefing/presentation/README]].

## Talk revision 6

Muk then asked to shorten the presentation, with about ten slides being enough and "super short". The notebook read that as ten slides in the file and cut the talk to about six minutes. See [[Designs/Paper2Agent Briefing/presentation/README]].

## Talk revision 7

Muk pasted a design review and asked to keep the core ideas and not lose the plot. The notebook rebuilt the deck with native PowerPoint shapes (build_pptx.py) in an indigo and teal palette, with amber used only for limits. Positions kept against the review: (1) the before-and-after panels are folded into the worked-example slide, because a separate before-and-after slide would repeat it and Muk asked for about ten slides; (2) the coupling diagram stays in the main talk as slide 7, because Muk asked for a diagram showing where leakage and methodology issues appear, and the review moved limits to backup; (3) the three metrics keep their denominators and the "not re-checked" source line, because the numbers are still unverified; (4) the pipeline uses the repository's six stages with mount-and-run folded into Check, and the fresh verifier's repair ability stays visible on that stage. Core ideas kept: answer first (title slide), what it does, how it works, reported evidence, value, capsule fit, one limit, next step. See [[Designs/Paper2Agent Briefing/presentation/README]].

## Talk revision 8

Muk required the talk to start with the value of Paper2Agent to AI4Research, covering the value of the idea and the methodology without a deep dive, and not to diverge from the core intent. The core intent, from Muk's first request, is a short talk on the value of Paper2Agent and its connection to AI4Research, with a workflow diagram and a diagram of where leakage and methodology issues appear. Revision 8 puts value on slide 2 and keeps every other slide serving value, how it works, connection or the one limit. See [[Designs/Paper2Agent Briefing/presentation/README]].

## Capability Capsule Factory integration

Muk's [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-capability-factory-integration.txt|latest visible message]] asks to integrate the Factory document and ensure the documents are in good condition. The pasted recap reports that rev8 was copied to Desktop and suggests a one-page feature definition as a next step; those are prior assistant words, not a new factory admission, ownership assignment, or implementation result. We treat Muk's request as authorization to integrate the existing proposal into the notebook reading route and presentation while keeping the detailed plan intact.

[[Designs/Capability Capsule Factory Feature]] is the short entrypoint. [[Designs/Paper2Agent Capability Factory]] remains the stage/source note; [[Designs/AI4Research Capability Growth]] covers architecture changes and whole-system improvement. Revision 9 of the talk makes the three identities visible (factory stages, generated product CCs, resources) and keeps two boundaries explicit: generated products need independent admission, and acquisition needs a separately authorized preparation path because our M1 intake cannot clone from the internet. The proposed flat pilot and future composite are distinguished. This is documentation alignment, not an M1 design amendment or runtime evidence.

## Latest infrastructure direction

The exact [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-vm-capsule-factory-design-plan.txt|request]] sets full CC containerization as the design target. Every VM owns an evolving AI4R, factory and Docker images. Projects evolve independently; selected CC/platform improvements may return to the tested master base. Expected low overhead is a working assumption to measure. [[Designs/Lab Capsule and Factory Design Plan]] defines next work. This supersedes optional-carrier framing while preserving the bounded pilot and current M1 authority.

## M2 scope correction and Docker review

The [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-m2-scope-clarification.txt|user correction]] places all VM/container/factory architecture in M2. M1 constraints describe the starting baseline, not M2 exclusions or a gate on design. [[Designs/Docker Architecture Review and Feasibility]] reviews actual Docker mechanics; [[Designs/Lab Capsule and Factory Design Plan]] now contains concrete provisioning and development slices.

## Spark constraints and shared resources

The exact [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-21-spark-resource-and-storage-direction.txt|request]] fixes the development machine as a 128 GB unified-memory GB10 Spark with a 2 TB SSD and reports observed image bloat on another machine. Models/inference should be central rather than duplicated per VM. [[Designs/Spark Shared Services and Storage]] defines shared acquisition, bounded working sets, capacity reservations and retained provenance during factory/generalist iteration. No Spark measurement is implied.

## M1 coder prompt and notebook placement

The user asks for a small, portable filename-only M1 benchmark/CI prompt closely matching the existing design. It now lives at [[Resources/Coder Prompts/benchmark-runner]], separate from M2 architecture. The original root file was moved; coder-facing text is unchanged. The user-reported working M1 machine is a separate source of runtime evidence, not proof that this workstation or the Spark has passed qualification.

## Standalone, human-judged benchmark-runner

The initial standalone interpretation of the three requests is superseded by [[Sources/Prompts/2026-10-22-benchmark-runner-branch-and-runtime-safety.txt|the branch/runtime clarification]]. [[Resources/Coder Prompts/benchmark-runner]] belongs in our AI4Research branch of JiuwenSwarm. Normal authorized development changes there are allowed; the protection applies during benchmark execution, which must never overwrite user files or mutate original projects/live state. It selects isolated branches/targets, runs cases in parallel and exports evidence for human judgment. Developers and developer-commanded agents are authorized callers. Automatic external scoring and M2 lab/factory implementation are not required. Codex authentication remains an explicit human setup dependency.
